// Where one edge crosses the sample line, and which way.
ras_cross(int i, int y, int[] tab) {
  int v = ras_hits((import rasy0)[i], (import rasy1)[i], y);
  if(v != 0) {
    ras_add(i, y, tab, v);
  }
  int ret_i = i + 1;
} return int ret_i;

// A half-open test -- the top of an edge counts, the bottom does not -- so
// that two edges meeting at a point are counted once between them and not
// twice or not at all. This is also the whole of the nonzero winding rule's
// input: an edge running downwards contributes +1 and one running upwards
// -1, and TrueType winds a hole the opposite way from the shape around it,
// which is what makes the counter of an 'o' cancel to zero. Even-odd would
// agree about an 'o' and disagree about any glyph whose contours overlap,
// which composites produce routinely.
ras_hits(int lo, int hi, int y) {
  int v = 0;
  if(lo <= y && y < hi) {
    v = 1;
  } else if(hi <= y && y < lo) {
    v = 0 - 1;
  }
} return int v;

// The crossing is recorded as a difference: +1 or -1 at the column where the
// edge cuts the line, and the prefix sum in ras_scan turns the whole row's
// differences back into a winding number per column. That is what removes
// the sort -- crossings may arrive in any order, and addition does not care.
//
// A crossing off the left of the bitmap is clamped onto column 0, which is
// correct: the span it opens covers everything from the left edge. One off
// the right is clamped onto the spare cell past the end, which the prefix
// sum never reaches, and is equally correct.
ras_add(int i, int y, int[] tab, int v) {
  int n = ras_add_col(i, y);
  lset(tab, n, tab[n] + v);
} return void;
